# Bug Bounty Writeup-Email Verification Bypass Vulnerability

## Introduction

Email verification is used in web applications to confirm that a user really owns the email address used during registration.

In this vulnerability, the application incorrectly verified the old email address even after the user changed the account email during signup.

Because of this flaw, an attacker could create an account using another person’s email address without having access to that email inbox.

---

# What Gets Affected?

This vulnerability affects:

- User registration system
- Email verification process
- Account identity validation
- Authentication trust

It can impact:

- User accounts
- Business workflows
- Admin approval systems
- Email-based security checks

---

# Affected Component

## Email Verification System

The email verification system is responsible for:

- sending verification links
- validating email ownership
- activating user accounts securely

In this case:

- the verification token was not updated after email change
- old verification links still remained valid

This caused the application to trust the wrong email address.

---

# How It Was Exploited

## Step 1 — Register with Attacker Email

The attacker registers using:

```
attacker@example.com
```

The application sends a verification link to the attacker inbox.

---

## Step 2 — Change Email Address

Before verifying, the attacker changes the email to:

```
victim@example.com
```

---

## Step 3 — Old Verification Link Still Works

The application does not:

- invalidate the old token
- generate a new verification link

The attacker still has access to the original verification email.

---

## Step 4 — Verify Using Old Email

The attacker clicks the old verification link received in:

```
attacker@example.com
```

The application accepts the verification.

---

## Final Result

The account becomes registered as:

```
victim@example.com
```

even though the victim never verified the email.

---

# Impact

An attacker could:

- Create accounts using emails they do not own
- Impersonate other users
- Abuse company email registrations
- Block legitimate users from signing up
- Break trust in the authentication system

---

# Root Cause

| Issue | Explanation |
| --- | --- |
| Old token not invalidated | Previous verification link still active |
| No new verification token | New email never properly verified |
| Weak backend validation | Application trusted old verification state |
| Improper session handling | Email and verification became mismatched |

---

# Mitigation

To fix this vulnerability:

- Invalidate old verification tokens after email changes
- Generate a completely new verification link
- Send verification only to the updated email address
- Prevent account creation until new email is verified
- Revalidate email ownership whenever email is modified

---

# Security Lesson

Applications should always bind verification tokens to the current email address.

Any email change during registration must:

- invalidate previous verification sessions
- restart the verification process securely

Otherwise attackers may bypass email ownership validation.
